  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1202 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe blast of furnace heat shows no signs of abating the remainder of \nthe week. The upper (H5) high holds strong in the upper 590s DM \nwhile gradually retrograding across the FA. H8 temps actually \nincrease 2-3C during this period, resulting in forecast surface \nhighs challenging mid September record warmth both in terms of \nabsolute maximum but also some record warm minimums are in play each \nday the remainder of the week. Combined with dews holding pretty \nfirmly in the upper 60s-lower 70s over most of that time, resultant \ntriple digit heat indices will too.\n\nWith the upper high so strong and influential, our only short term \nchance at appreciable rainfall is with rimfire storms. The nearest\nsuch chance is late today into the evening, with a front that is\npositioned off to the north. If some of its heat-of-day \nevolution can blow off down as far as I-64, this might offer the\nbest chance at an isolated exception to what will otherwise\ncontinue as basically day-after-day dry conditions.\n\nThe next best chance thereafter is with some late weekend ridge\nbreakdown as a cold front makes its play toward the region. Its\npassage brings cooler temperatures and less humid conditions \nfor early next week; enjoy that while we can as a resurging \nsignal for broad upper ridging over the country's mid section \nputs our 6-10 and 8-14 projections back into \"Above Normal\" for \ntemperatures, as if summer just does not want to yield, autumnal\nequinox or not.",
